{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "Evidence: canary 1.1 served 2384 of 9788 requests (~25%) with 0 5xx (0.00%), identical to stable's 0/7404. Aggregate latency is equal or better than stable: p50 33ms vs 35ms, p95 95ms vs 95ms. Per-endpoint, /browse (p50 22/p95 27/p99 30 vs 22/27/28) and /checkout (79/102/105 vs 82/103/106) match stable, so the money path is clean. The over-time table shows no drift or degradation: canary p95 95/95/92/95ms across the four 30s windows vs stable 96/93/94/97ms, with zero 5xx on both sides in every window. The one regression is /search p99: 652ms on canary vs 61ms on stable, which also pushes aggregate canary p99 to 172ms vs 104ms. Judgement: this is an extreme-tail-only effect, not a broad regression - canary /search p95 is 60ms vs stable 59ms, so only roughly the top 1% of 729 canary search requests (about 7 requests) is slow, which is a noisy, low-confidence estimate at that sample size, and it did not grow over the 120s test (no sign of a leak, queue build-up, or cache/connection-pool saturation). With zero errors, no p50/p95 regression on any endpoint, no adverse trend, and the blip on a read-only endpoint rather than checkout, blocking the release is not justified. Promote, but do not treat it as clean: after promotion watch /search p99 at full traffic, add an alert on /search p99 above ~200ms, and be ready to roll back if the tail widens or moves into p95 - at 100% traffic a real tail defect would affect ~4x more requests than this canary measured."
}

{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "Most signals are clean: canary 1.1 served 2384 of 9788 requests (~24%, matching the 1-of-4-pod split) with 0 5xx (0.00% vs stable 0.00%), p50 33 ms vs 35 ms, p95 95 ms vs 95 ms, and the 30 s windows show no drift or warm-up effect (canary p95 95/95/92/95 vs stable 96/93/94/97, zero 5xx in every window). /browse and /checkout match stable at every percentile (browse p99 30 vs 28; checkout p50 79 vs 82, p95 102 vs 103, p99 105 vs 106).\n\nThe blocker is one real tail regression: canary /search p99 = 652 ms vs stable 61 ms (~10x), which also drives canary overall p99 to 172 ms vs stable 104 ms. This is not sampling noise. Stable's entire /search distribution over 2273 requests is tightly bounded (p50 47, p95 59, p99 61 ms), so under stable's behaviour the expected number of requests above ~100 ms in the canary's 729 /search calls is well under one; a p99 of 652 ms implies roughly 7+ requests at or above that level. Canary /search p95 (60 ms) still matches stable (59 ms), so the slow path hits only about the slowest 1% of searches - severe but narrow.\n\nJudgement: an unexplained new ~10x slow path on a user-facing endpoint (typical causes: a missing index, an N+1 query, lock contention or a GC/cold-cache path introduced in 1.1) is the kind of defect that grows with traffic and data volume, and 25% exposure for 120 s is too small a sample to bound it. Given the stated asymmetry - a bad promote hurts customers, a bad rollback only delays a release - the cheaper error is to hold. Roll back, get a trace or profile of the slow /search requests to find the cause, then re-canary. If the cause turns out to be benign and bounded (e.g. one-off cache fill), promoting with a /search p99 alert would be acceptable; without that explanation, do not take it to 100%."
}
